<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Anhalt</h1><p class="st-meta">1212 CE – 1603 CE · capital Dessau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ascanian principality between the Harz and the Elbe that adopted the Reformation early and was repeatedly divided among branches of its ruling house.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Henry I, son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-duke-of-saxony">Bernhard of Saxony</a> of the Ascanian house, received the family's ancestral lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> and middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> when his father's possessions were divided in 1212. He took the title Count, then Prince, of Anhalt, after the castle of that name near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harzgerode">Harzgerode</a>, founding a principality separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Anhalt was repeatedly split among branches, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschersleben">Aschersleben</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, each ruling small towns and farmland along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-prince-of-anhalt-kothen">Prince Wolfgang of Anhalt-Köthen</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a> in 1530, making Anhalt an early Lutheran land, and it later turned Reformed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ernest-prince-of-anhalt">Joachim Ernst</a> reunited the territories in 1570 and issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> code.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ernest-prince-of-anhalt">Joachim Ernst</a>'s death, his sons divided Anhalt in 1603 into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Anhalt-Dessau</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-bernburg">Anhalt-Bernburg</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-kothen">Anhalt-Köthen</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">Anhalt-Zerbst</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-plotzkau">Anhalt-Plötzkau</a>. These lines were reunited as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a> in 1863, which became a free state in 1918 and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-anhalt.jpg" alt="Principality of Anhalt" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Herzogtum_Anhalt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1212 CE – 1603 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dessau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry I of Anhalt, Wolfgang of Anhalt-Köthen, Joachim Ernst of Anhalt</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism and Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anhalt</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt" aria-label="Principality of Anhalt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4956 385 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Anhalt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5276 641L5229 694M5276 641L5276 641M5276 641L5265 642M5276 641L5270 644M5276 641L5272 638M5276 641L5264 644"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5276 